WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.077 --> 00:00:02.593
(soil tapping)

00:00:02.593 --> 00:00:05.176
(gentle music)

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:10.770
- My name is Darren.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:15.210
I would describe myself
as a bonsai practitioner.

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:18.540
I'm going to walk you through
how I restore to health

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:21.180
a hemlock bonsai tree.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:22.250
As I look at the tree,

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:26.550
I can see straightaway that
there are a couple of issues

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:27.560
surrounding its health.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:31.230
First of all, the foliage is off-green.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:33.730
It should be a nice medium
to deep green color.

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:35.420
As I look at the soil,

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:38.010
we can see that it's
mounded up quite a lot,

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:41.150
and it looks very black, very dense.

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:44.290
All of these signs combined
tell me that the tree

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:47.773
is suffering due to
some kind of root issue.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:56.980
Now that I'm repotting this bonsai tree,

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:59.680
I need to remove it from the pot,

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:02.160
and when a tree's been
in a pot for this long,

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.173
it's quite a task in and of itself.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:06.390
To start with though,

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:10.260
I'm going to remove some of the topsoil.

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:14.230
I'm searching for structural roots.

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:17.150
In bonsai terms, we call it nebari.

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:19.260
And what that is, is, if
you ever look at a tree,

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:23.030
they've got kind of thick
roots gripping the ground.

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:25.520
That's a valuable feature
of the bonsai tree.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:29.340
It will give me clues as to
the depth of the root ball,

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:32.363
where the tree ends and
where the root system begins.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.233
Now I need to free up
the edges of the pot.

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:43.960
To do that, I'm gonna use
a tool that in Japanese

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:45.760
is called a kama, or in the West

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:47.713
we might call it a repotting sickle.

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:53.850
I'm using the kama to
scrape away matted roots

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:57.440
and compacted soil from
the edges of the container.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:59.610
That's going to give me
a little bit more room

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:04.610
to use the leverage of the
trunk when trying to extract it.

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:08.780
It also frees any roots that
have adhered to the side

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:09.940
of the pot

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:12.913
so that the tree's no longer
really gripping onto it.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:30.690
Now that it's out of the pot,

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:33.210
I'm going to turn the tree over

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:35.850
and have look at what we've got.

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:39.330
And I'm actually blown
away by the amount of root

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:42.220
that has developed
underneath this root ball.

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:45.460
The roots must be a couple
of centimeters thick.

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:47.963
There's no soil in and
amongst these roots.

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:52.120
I'm going to use a simple paint
scraper that I've sharpened

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:53.630
so that it's got a nice edge

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:58.480
and slice through roughly where
I think the matted roots end

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:01.243
and the actual old soil begins.

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:13.440
Once I've removed that big,
thick mass of unwanted roots,

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.290
I need to work my way through the soil.

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:20.590
I'm searching for any broken-down soil

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:23.630
or any mucky organic matter

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:26.560
that doesn't support the right
balance of water and oxygen

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:28.530
for healthy roots.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:31.630
To do that, I'm going to be
using a bamboo chopstick,

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:34.530
which I've sharpened to a point.

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:38.140
The bamboo chopstick is able
to work soil from the roots

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:39.793
while doing minimal damage.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.290
I've also got my root shears,

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:46.690
which allow me to cut away any roots

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:49.470
that are clearly not needed.

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:52.620
(shears snipping)

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:54.690
As I'm working through the soil,

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:58.460
I eventually come to what
I've been dreading to find,

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:01.440
old field soil or whatever it is.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:03.550
Definitely don't want it in a bonsai pot.

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:05.920
It hinders the passage of water and oxygen

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:07.000
through the roots,

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.380
which can affect the health
of the tree moving forward.

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:14.633
I have to use my chopstick and
my sheers to dig the lot out.

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:23.990
Now that I've completed the remedial works

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:25.140
to this root ball,

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:28.583
I need to seat the tree
into the container.

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:31.990
The first step of doing
that is to prepare my pot.

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:39.310
I'm now inserting what we
call an aeration layer of soil

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:40.800
into the bottom of the pot.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.720
This allows air to enter the pot

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:45.500
from the drainage holes beneath

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:47.493
and permeate through the root ball.

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:51.940
The soil that I'm using
is a mix of akadama,

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:53.990
pumice, and lava rocks,

00:04:53.990 --> 00:04:57.343
which I'm using in a 1:1:1 ratio.

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:02.360
In order to seat the tree in the container

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:05.690
and minimize the risk of air pockets,

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:07.440
I'm going to make a mound of soil

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:10.873
roughly where the hollow
section is underneath the trunk.

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:16.723
And I'm now seating the
tree onto that mound.

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:30.000
Now that the tree's
seated in the container,

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.793
I need to secure it to the pot.

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:36.070
I'm using some small sections of bamboo

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:40.280
pushed into the root ball to
secure the tree into its pot.

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:44.040
I tie the wires, which wrap
underneath the container,

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:45.333
to those stakes.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:50.160
Doing so is going to prevent
any risk of the tree swaying

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:53.693
or moving, and therefore
damaging newly forming roots.

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:01.550
Now that this tree is secure
in its pot, it can't move.

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:02.750
It can't go anywhere

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:12.530
And the repot's complete.

